# ============================================================
#  Expansion — Final year: careers, last times, farewells.
#  career_* flags (set by the Career Fair) + career_accepted
#  feed two endings: ending_teacher, ending_wanderer.
# ============================================================

@event y3_career_fair
title: The Career Fair
class: story
tone: light
trigger: auto
when: final_year and month == 2
priority: 42
type: menu
art: scroll
tags: study story
chain: careers
scene: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.
text: Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?
> conclave: The Conclave of the Seven Towers
  hint: Apprentice archmages. The interview tests your Arcana.
  flag career_conclave
  queue y3_interview now
  say: You leave your name with the mage in silver, who writes it down without looking up. The interview is set.
> library: The Royal Library
  hint: Keepers of every book ever written. The interview tests your Lore.
  flag career_library
  queue y3_interview now
  say: The librarian takes your name and asks what you're reading at the moment. You answer. The interview is set.
> guild: The Guild of Alchemists
  hint: Potions, profit, explosions. The interview tests your Alchemy.
  flag career_guild
  queue y3_interview now
  say: The alchemist at the guild's booth shakes your hand and checks it for burns. The interview is set.
> unseen: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs
  hint: They found you, actually. The interview tests your Guile.
  flag career_unseen
  queue y3_interview now
  say: A woman nobody else seems to notice takes your name and tells you where to be. The interview is set.
> circus: The Travelling Circus of Wonders
  hint: Wagons, crowds, illusions. The interview tests your Charm.
  flag career_circus
  queue y3_interview now
  say: The ringmaster juggles your name into the air, catches it and writes it down. The interview is set.
> teaching: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher
  hint: Needs a faculty member who trusts you: Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress.
  when: rel(thorne) >= 15 or rel(maud) >= 15 or rel(isolde) >= 15
  locked: Nobody on the faculty knows you well enough to vouch for you.
  flag career_teaching
  queue y3_interview now
  say: Your old professor puts your name forward to the faculty, with a few words you don't hear. The interview is set.
> none: Not yet — you'd rather not decide
  mood +tiny
  if month < 4:
    queue y3_career_fair in 1
  say: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. {month < 4: The future can wait one more month; the fair is back next month.|The fair packs up for good. The future will have to come and find you.}

@event y3_interview
title: The Interview
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
art: scales
tags: study story
chain: careers
scene: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.
text: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. "Tell us," says the smiling one, "why you."
> left: Let your record speak
  iv_score = 0
  challenge research adept:
    win:
      iv_score += 1
      queue y3_interview_practical now
      say: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's "yes".
    lose:
      mood -small
      queue y3_interview_practical now
      say: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. "Now," says the smiling one, "a small practical."
> right: Tell them a story
  iv_score = 0
  challenge negotiate adept wild:
    win:
      iv_score += 1
      queue y3_interview_practical now
      say: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.
    lose:
      mood -small
      queue y3_interview_practical now
      say: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself. "Now," says the smiling one, "a small practical."

@event y3_interview_practical
title: The Practical
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: queued
art: scales
tags: study story
chain: careers
scene: A plain table in a panelled room set with a single task, three interviewers watching a student roll up their sleeves.
text: The note-taker sets a task on the table. {flag(career_conclave): A ward with a flaw in it, somewhere.|{flag(career_library): A crumbling manuscript in three hands and no catalogue.|{flag(career_guild): A cauldron, a recipe with one ingredient missing, and a clock.|{flag(career_unseen): A sealed letter, and the request to read it without breaking the seal.|{flag(career_circus): An empty stage, a sceptical crowd of three, and five minutes.|A class of imaginary first-years, and a lesson to give them.}}}}} "In your own time," says the smiling one, and turns over an hourglass.
> left: Do it properly
  if flag(career_conclave):
    challenge spell expert drift low:
      win:
        iv_score += 1
        say: You find the flaw, name it and close it, and the ward rings like a struck glass. The silent one leans forward for the first time.
      lose:
        mood -small
        say: The flaw finds you first. The ward fizzes out in your hands, and the note-taker writes for a long time.
  elif flag(career_library):
    challenge research expert drift low:
      win:
        iv_score += 1
        say: Three hands, three centuries, one lost chapter. You date them all before the sand runs out. The silent one nods, once.
      lose:
        mood -small
        say: You date the second hand a century wrong, and know it the moment you say it. The note-taker writes for a long time.
  elif flag(career_guild):
    challenge brew expert drift low:
      win:
        iv_score += 1
        say: You find the missing ingredient by its absence and the cauldron settles to a perfect simmer. Someone behind the table sniffs, approvingly.
      lose:
        mood -small
        say: The cauldron spits and turns a colour no recipe asks for. The note-taker writes for a long time.
  elif flag(career_unseen):
    challenge deceive expert drift low:
      win:
        iv_score += 1
        say: Steam, a thin blade, a little patience. You read it, reseal it and hand it back. The silent one smiles, which is somehow worse.
      lose:
        mood -small
        say: The seal cracks with a sound like a shot. The silent one does not look surprised. The note-taker writes for a long time.
  elif flag(career_circus):
    challenge negotiate expert drift low:
      win:
        iv_score += 1
        say: Five minutes of nonsense and wonder, and the crowd of three are on their feet. One of them is the silent one.
      lose:
        mood -small
        say: The crowd of three stays seated, arms folded, for all five minutes. The note-taker writes for a long time.
  else:
    challenge negotiate using lore expert drift low:
      win:
        iv_score += 1
        say: You teach the empty chairs the thing you most wish someone had taught you, and forget for a while that they're empty. The silent one nods.
      lose:
        mood -small
        say: You lose the imaginary class in the first minute, which you hadn't known was possible. The note-taker writes for a long time.
  queue y3_interview_question now
> right: Ask questions before you start
  mood -tiny
  iv_score += 1
  if iv_score >= 2:
    say: You ask three good questions, then do the task carefully and well. It's slower than they'd like. The silent one doesn't seem to mind.
  else:
    iv_score -= 1
    say: You ask three good questions and run out of sand before you've finished. "Careful," says the smiling one, in a tone that isn't quite praise.
  queue y3_interview_question now

@event y3_interview_question
title: The Silent One's Question
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: queued
art: scales
tags: study story
chain: careers
scene: A small panelled room, an interviewer who hasn't spoken leaning forward over folded hands, a student on the other side of the table very still.
text: At last the silent one speaks. "Tell me about a time you did something you weren't proud of," they say, "and whether you'd do it again."
> left: Tell the truth
  alignment +tiny
  if alignment >= 20:
    iv_score += 1
    say: You tell them the truth, all of it, and what you learned, and that you wouldn't. The silent one is quiet for a long moment. "Thank you," they say, and it sounds like the end of a search.
  else:
    say: You tell them the truth, and halfway through you realise you might do it again. So does the silent one. "Honest," they say. It isn't clear whether that's good.
  queue y3_interview_verdict now
> right: Tell them what they want to hear
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive expert drift low:
    win:
      iv_score += 1
      say: A small mistake, gracefully regretted, neatly learned from. It isn't quite true. The silent one writes something down and underlines it.
    lose:
      iv_score -= 1
      mood -small
      say: The story is too neat, and the silent one has heard it before, from better liars. "I see," they say, and you hear the door closing.
  queue y3_interview_verdict now

@event y3_interview_verdict
title: We'll Be in Touch
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: queued
art: letter
tags: study story
chain: careers
scene: A student walking out of a panelled room into a bright corridor, loosening their collar, a closed door behind them.
text: "We'll be in touch," says the smiling one, and stands, and so you stand too. In the corridor your legs feel like someone else's. {iv_score >= 3: You've never been so sure of anything.|{iv_score >= 2: You think it went well. You think.|{iv_score >= 1: It could have gone either way.|You know exactly how it went.}}}
> ok: Wait for the letter
  if iv_score >= 2:
    flag career_offered
    queue y3_offer_letter in 2
    say: You go back to your room and lie on the bed and don't move for an hour. Somewhere, three people are deciding.
  elif iv_score >= 1:
    queue y3_waitlist in 2
    say: You go back to your room and replay every answer. The better ones get better each time; the bad ones get worse.
  else:
    queue y3_rejection in 2
    say: You go back to your room and don't replay anything at all. Some things you just know.

@event y3_waitlist
title: The Waiting List
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: queued
art: letter
tags: story
chain: careers
scene: A thin envelope on a dormitory desk, a single page beside it, a student reading it a second time with their chin on their fist.
text: A thin letter. You are "a strong candidate" and on "a short list", and they "hope to write again". It's not a no. It's not anything.
> left: Write back and make your case
  challenge negotiate journeyman+ drift low:
    win:
      flag career_offered
      queue y3_offer_letter in 1
      say: Two pages, plain and sure: why you, why them, why now. A fortnight later, a heavier envelope.
    lose:
      mood -small
      say: You write two pages, then four, then a fifth you shouldn't send, and do. No heavier envelope comes.
> right: Let it lie and see
  mood -tiny
  chance unlikely:
    flag career_offered
    queue y3_offer_letter in 2
    say: You let it lie. Two months later, when you'd almost stopped looking, the heavier envelope comes after all.
  else:
    say: You let it lie. It stays lying. Other paths open; you try not to look back at this one.

@event y3_rejection
title: A Polite Letter
class: story
tone: tense
trigger: queued
art: letter
tags: story
chain: careers
scene: A crumpled letter on a windowsill, a student looking out at the lake with their arms folded.
text: A short letter, very politely written. They were "impressed by your enthusiasm". They regret. They wish you every success.
> left: Ask them what went wrong
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: You write and ask. To your surprise they answer, frankly, two pages of it. It stings, and it's the most useful thing anyone's told you all year.
> right: Throw it in the lake
  mood +small
  say: You fold it into a boat and launch it from the jetty. It sinks with great dignity. Some doors close; there are a great many doors.

@event y3_offer_letter
title: The Offer
class: story
tone: light
trigger: queued
when: flag(career_offered)
art: letter
tags: story
chain: careers
scene: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.
text: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. {iv_score >= 3: There is a signing purse, too, which they call "a token".|} It needs a signature by the end of the month.
> left: Sign it today
  flag career_accepted
  mood +medium
  if iv_score >= 3:
    gold +medium
  say: Your signature looks strange on the page — like a grown-up's. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.
> right: Ask them to wait
  mood -tiny
  lore +tiny
  say: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly. The future, it turns out, does not like waiting, but it will.

@event y3_first_year_mentee
title: Your First-Year
class: routine
tone: light
when: final_year and month <= 4
weight: 5
art: feather
tags: social
scene: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.
text: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including "are the stairs alive" and "are YOU alive".
> left: Answer every question honestly
  mood +small
  charm +small
  say: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not "friendly". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.
> right: Teach Pim to find answers alone
  lore +small
  say: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.

@event y3_last_lanterns
time: evening night
title: The Last Lantern Night
class: routine
tone: light
when: final_year and month == 2
weight: 40
art: lantern
tags: festival
scene: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.
text: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.
> left: Wish for the people you'll leave behind
  mood +medium
  say: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.
> right: Leave it blank
  lore +tiny
  mood +tiny
  say: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.

@event y3_thesis
title: The Last Chapter
class: story
tone: light
when: final_year and month >= 5 and month <= 8 and member(thesis)
weight: 16
art: book
tags: study
scene: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.
text: Your thesis needs one more chapter. You could write something safe and solid, or chase the question your sources keep circling and nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.
> left: Safe and solid
  progress thesis +medium
  say: Forty pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.
> right: The question nobody answered
  challenge research expert wild:
    win:
      progress thesis +large
      renown +medium
      say: The chapter is so good that your supervisor shows it round the staff room, and the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.
    lose:
      progress thesis +tiny
      mood -small
      say: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. The chapter ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.

@event y3_portrait
title: Sitting for the Portrait
class: routine
tone: light
when: final_year and month >= 7
weight: 5
art: mirror
tags: story
scene: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.
text: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.
> left: Pose solemn and dignified
  renown +small
  say: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.
> right: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh
  mood +medium
  charm +tiny
  say: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.

@event y3_letter_to_self
title: A Letter to Your First-Year Self
class: routine
tone: warm
when: final_year and month >= 4
weight: 10
art: letter
tags: home
scene: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.
text: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.
> left: Tell them it'll be all right
  chance likely:
    mood +medium
    say: You write: "It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right." The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.
  else:
    mood -tiny
    say: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.
> right: Warn them about everything
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: "Some things must be learned the hard way."

@event y3_maud_seeds
title: Seeds for Later
class: windfall
tone: light
when: final_year and month >= 9 and met(maud) and rel(maud) >= 9
actor: maud
weight: 5
art: flower
tags: nature social
scene: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.
text: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. "Seeds," she says. "From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them." She sniffs. "Like you did."
> left: Hug her
  rel maud +large
  mood +medium
  say: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. "Go on," she says. "Before I get soppy."
> right: Promise to come back and visit
  rel maud +medium
  flag promised_maud
  say: "Every summer," you promise. "We'll see," she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.

@event y3_last_duel
time: morning
title: One Last Duel
class: routine
tone: tense
when: final_year and month >= 6 and (member(duelling) or flag(tournament_champion)) and met(lyra)
actor: lyra
weight: 16
art: duel
tags: rival
scene: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.
text: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. "No referee," she says. "No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know."
> left: Duel her with everything
  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana +2:
    win:
      rel lyra +medium
      vigor +small
      say: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. "Now we know," she says.
    lose:
      rel lyra +medium
      health -medium
      say: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. "Now we know."
> right: Lower your wand and just talk
  study -tiny
  rel lyra +large
  if spark(lyra) >= 20:
    spark lyra +small
  say: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. You both miss the morning lecture. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.

@event y3_time_capsule
title: The Time Capsule
class: windfall
tone: light
when: final_year and month >= 8 and bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 2
weight: 5
art: key
tags: social
scene: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.
text: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?
> left: Something precious
  mood +medium
  say: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.
> right: Something ridiculous
  mood +large
  say: You put in a single sock, labelled "EVIDENCE". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.

@event y3_speech
title: The Graduation Speech
class: story
tone: light
trigger: auto
when: turn == max_turns and (renown >= 40 or gpa >= 3.0)
priority: 66
actor: isolde
art: crown
tags: story
scene: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.
text: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. "Each year one graduate speaks for their class," she says. "This year, the faculty would like it to be you."
> left: Accept and write something honest
  renown +large
  mood +medium
  rel isolde +small
  say: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.
> right: Decline — someone else deserves it
  rel isolde +small
  charm +tiny
  say: "Interesting," says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.
